WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Can sampling edges change an instability decision?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
110 draws retain 67 of the 99 original edges.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:20.000
A single mode makes the false alarm visible.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Every nonconstant original mode lies below the band.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The largest sampled mode lands inside the band, reversing the verdict.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The two marked values come from the same frozen comparison.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Why this interval? Start with the equation for a single mode.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Original: no strictly unstable mode. Sample: an unstable mode.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Two states per vertex evolve through reaction and graph diffusion.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.000
A graph eigenvalue q reduces the dynamics to one 2 × 2 block.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The trace stays negative for q ≥ 0. The determinant changes sign at two roots.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.000
A negative determinant forces one positive eigenvalue.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:38.000
As q enters (2,5), the growth rate crosses zero.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
At a band endpoint the largest growth rate is exactly zero.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
The endpoints are neutral. Outside the band this model has no positive growth.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The animation scans q; a particular graph supplies discrete modes.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Effective-resistance sampling weights probability by edge weight times resistance.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Inverse-probability weighting applies to each draw. The first lights a single edge.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Repeated draws add weight to the same edge; vertex positions stay fixed.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.000
The replay advances to the 20th draw of the same frozen sequence.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.000
After 110 draws, the highlighted network contains the 67 retained edges.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Now scan a weighted blend of the two Laplacians as a separate illustration.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Recompute the spectrum along the blend. The largest mode crosses q = 2.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Only the endpoints are the graphs in this frozen comparison.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
First bound the two quadratic forms in every direction.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The min–max principle brackets each ordered original eigenvalue.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Hold the sampled eigenvalue fixed and vary a hypothetical ε.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:37.000
The two denominators make the enclosure widen asymmetrically.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.000
At the false-alarm distortion, the interval crosses both edges of the band.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
The sampled point is in the band, but the original mode is unresolved.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Switch to another frozen row and enlarge the 0–6 range.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:06.000
One entire interval inside the band certifies an unstable original mode.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.000
The third frozen row uses all 23 nonconstant-mode intervals.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:22.000
The certificate excludes strictly unstable modes only when every interval avoids the band.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
The draw count is matched in each column. Distinct-edge counts can differ.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
At 110 draws, mean retained-edge fractions are 68.0% and 71.2%.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Verdict disagreements at 110 draws: 63 for effective resistance and 57 for uniform.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:54.000
The ordering reverses at 350 draws. One budget cannot settle a general ranking.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:02.000
With that same budget, neither method yields a conclusive certificate.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:11.000
At 700 draws nearly all edges remain, yet fewer than half the certificates conclude.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:19.000
Six replicates × three graph seeds give 18 base samples.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Reusing each sample at ten scales gives 180 dependent evaluations.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:38.000
False alarms and misses have different denominators. Zero misses describes this cell only.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:46.000
The reference implementation computes ε on the subspace orthogonal to constants.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:55.000
The distortion uses the original decomposition, so this audit establishes no end-to-end speedup.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:04.000
The interval logic assumes a valid sandwich. The experiment checks eigenvalues in floating point.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:13.000
The interval rule has three possible outcomes.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:22.000
An inconclusive result preserves uncertainty about the original graph.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:31.000
This synthetic audit ends at a linear mode decision.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:40.000
To decide about the original graph, check where the intervals land.
